      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B in the rectangular prism, we need to use the three-dimensional version of the Pythagorean Theorem. This theorem states that in a rectangular prism, the length of the diagonal \\(d\\) can be found using the formula:\n\n\\[d = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}\\]\n\nwhere \\(l\\), \\(w\\), and \\(h\\) are the length, width, and height of the rectangular prism, respectively.\n\nIn this problem, the dimensions of the rectangular prism are 10-inches for the length, 20-inches for the width, and 10-inches for the height. We can substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[d = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2}\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the squares of the dimensions:\n\n\\[10^2 = 100\\]\n\\[20^2 = 400\\]\n\\[10^2 = 100\\]\n\nNext, we sum these squares:\n\n\\[100 + 400 + 100 = 600\\]\n\nFinally, we take the square root of the sum:\n\n\\[d = \\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{100 \\times 6} = \\sqrt{100} \\times \\sqrt{6} = 10\\sqrt{6}\\]\n\nThus, the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\\]",
